===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|urbanitie}}, from {{der|en|frm|urbanité}}, from {{der|en|la|urbānitās}}, from {{m|la|urbānus||belonging to a city}}, with a sense of "having the manners of townspeople" in [[w:Classical Latin|Classical Latin]], from {{m|la|urbs||city}}; equivalent to {{suffix|en|urban|ity}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/əːˈbæn.ɪ.ti/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-urbanity.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɝˈbæn.ə.ti/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[behaviour|Behaviour]] that is [[polished]], [[refined]], [[courteous]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1825|author=[[w:Washington Irving|Washington Irving]]|chapter=[[s:The Crayon Papers/Paris at the Restoration|Paris at the Restoration]]|title=The Crayon Papers
|passage=The vaunted courtesy of the old school, the smooth '''urbanity''' that prevailed in former days [...]}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|pages=261–262|volume=I|passage=He only stayed a few days in London, to take the oaths and his seat in the House of Lords, a ceremony that was to take place on the morrow, and he held himself much indebted to the circumstance of spending his first evening at Lady Anne's, because the marquis of Wentworthdale had, with the utmost '''urbanity''', offered to accompany him on that somewhat trying occasion,and he felt the value of his kindness not less than the courtesy evinced by a man of his rank and importance in the court and the House of Lords.}}
#* {{RQ:Le Fanu Wylder's Hand|chapter=A Threatening Notice|page=276|passage=Wealdon's two little visits explained perfectly the active '''urbanities''' of Captain Stanley Lake.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=The Stranger|page=115|passage=Eustace gaped at him in amazement. When his '''urbanity''' dropped away from him, as now, he had an innocence of expression which was almost infantile. It was as if the world had never touched him at all.}}
# [[urbanness|Urbanness]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1955|author=C.J. Lammers|title=Studies in Holland Flood Disaster 1953|volume=2|pageurl=http://books.google.ca/books?id=_DcrAAAAYAAJ&vq=urbanity&pg=PA39|page=39
|passage=[...], the majority of cases will differ as to "'''urbanity'''", as most of the evacuees were rural.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1956|author=Fred C. Iklé; Harry V. Kincaid|chapter=Social Aspects of Wartime Evacuation of American Cities|title=Disaster Study|volume=4|pageurl=http://books.google.ca/books?id=yTIrAAAAYAAJ&pg=RA2-PA44|page=44
|passage=Evacuees, the majority of whom were rural persons, reported more tensions as the '''urbanity''' of the reception community increased}}
#* {{quote-book|en|text=If the criterion of '''urbanity''' is the mixture of classes and ethic groups, in some cases including a mixture of blacks and whites, along with dens living and crowded streets and the omnipresence of all manner of business near the home in every quarter, then the cities of the United States in the years between 1820 and 1870 marked the zenith of our national '''urbanity'''.| title=The Urban Wilderness: A History of the American City| page=84| author=Sam Bass Warner| year=1995}}

